module.exports = [

{ order: 1, title: "The education option: what WJEC asks about schools",
content: `
<p>Education is Unit 2's largest option, and WJEC examines it with the same machinery as Unit 2's methods core: patterns of attainment, theories that explain them, processes inside schools, and the policies that structure the whole. The option's four heads are the map for this lesson. First, <strong>the role and functions of education</strong> — what school is for, in functionalist and Marxist accounts (pages 2–5). Second, <strong>differential achievement</strong> — the attainment gaps by class, gender and ethnicity, and the explanations offered for them (pages 6–11). Third, <strong>relationships and processes inside schools</strong> — labelling, expectations, setting and pupil subcultures (pages 8–9), which is where Unit 2's methods course pays its first dividend: every claim here was once a study you can now evaluate. Fourth, <strong>educational policy</strong> — comprehensivisation, marketisation and the reform era in England and Wales (pages 12–13).</p>
<p>Two ground rules set by the specification. The evidence base is overwhelmingly quantitative — official attainment statistics, cohort studies, the recurring census of results — so Lesson 9's discipline applies to nearly everything this chapter reports: the FSM gap, the gender gap and the ethnicity patterns are <em>recorded</em> differences, produced by definitions, measures and institutions (page 10 of Lesson 11 will be exercised three times in this lesson). And the theories are a continuation of Lesson 7's: functionalism and Marxism you have already met around deviance, now applied to the institution where the state and the young meet in person. Parsons's school is Durkheim's solidarity in a timetable; Althusser's classroom is the ruling ideology with a register; Willis's lads are Lesson 7's subcultures wearing uniform.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #1d4ed8;background:#eff6ff;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>The option's four heads:</strong> role and functions (pages 2–5) · differential achievement by class, gender and ethnicity (pages 6–11) · processes inside schools (pages 8–9) · educational policy in England and Wales (pages 12–13). The essay shapes for all of them are built on pages 14–15, and the methods course is exercised throughout.</p>
</div>
<p>Begin with the institution's own claim, because every theory in the chapter is an answer to it. Modern schooling is compulsory, universal and (in principle) meritocratic: whatever your home, the school offers the same curriculum, the same examinations, the same ladder. That claim — the answer to "what is school for?" — is what makes the attainment gaps so explosive: if the ladder is the same, the outcomes should not divide so exactly by the circumstances children are born into. The chapter's two theories are the two great readings of that contradiction: functionalism says the ladder works and the gaps measure effort and ability; Marxism says the ladder is the gap, dressed up as fairness. Everything else — the labels, the sets, the policies — is the detailed machinery by which one reading or the other turns out to be true.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) List the option's four heads with the pages that carry them. (b) Why is the FSM attainment gap "explosive" for the theory of schooling? (c) Show in one sentence each how Lesson 7's functionalism and Marxism reappear here.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Role and functions of education (pages 2\u20135: Durkheim, Parsons, Davis\u2013Moore; Althusser, Bowles\u2013Gintis, Willis); differential achievement by social class, gender and ethnicity (pages 6\u201311); relationships and processes inside schools \u2014 labelling, setting, subcultures (pages 8\u20139); educational policy, England and Wales (pages 12\u201313). (b) Because the institution\u2019s own justification is equal treatment for unequal homes \u2014 same curriculum, same exams, same ladder \u2014 so if outcomes then divide by the circumstances children are born into (entitlement to free school meals being the standing proxy for those circumstances), either the ladder is not actually equal in its effects or its theory of what it measures is wrong: the gap forces a verdict between the two great readings. (c) Functionalism: Durkheim\u2019s boundary-maintaining, solidarity-producing institution reappears as the school transmitting shared norms and specialist skills; Marxism: the ideology and apparatus that produced Lesson 7\u2019s working-class subcultures reappears as Althusser\u2019s ideological state apparatus \u2014 the same class machinery, met this time in uniform inside a classroom.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 2, title: "Functionalism I: Durkheim's solidarity and Parsons's bridge",
content: `
<p>Functionalism reads school as society's answer to a problem: how does a society of specialists, strangers and divided labour hold together and reproduce itself? Durkheim's answer runs through the two functions that carry his name in every specification. School transmits <strong>social solidarity</strong>: it teaches the shared norms — cooperation, punctuality, respect for impersonal rules — that family particularism cannot, because in a complex society the values that bind people must be common to all of them, not just one household's. And school teaches the <strong>specialist skills</strong> the division of labour requires: industrial society cannot run on the general competence of a village; it needs scientists, engineers, administrators and plumbers, produced in bulk and to a standard. Education, in Durkheim's phrase, is "the influence exercised by adult generations on those not yet ready for social life" — society socialising its replacements, methodically. This is Lesson 8's socialisation (Lesson 2's topic) made compulsory, universal and staffed.</p>
<p>Talcott Parsons added the functionalist image the examination quotes most: school as <strong>the bridge</strong>. The family socialises in the particularistic mode — the child is valued for being someone's child, loved whatever she achieves; adults are parents first and judges never. Wider society works in the universalistic mode — valued for what you achieve against shared standards, judged by rules that do not know your name. School is the institution that connects the two: it takes the family's particularistically loved child and teaches her to live under universalistic standards — achieved status gradually replacing ascribed, one rule for everyone, results that belong to her and not to her household. The hidden curriculum (page 9) is this bridge's building code: queues, bells, uniforms and registers are the machinery of universalism rehearsed daily.</p>
<p>Both accounts earn their keep as <em>descriptions</em>. Schools demonstrably do transmit norms (uniform, timetable, assembly — solidarity's toolkit in plain sight) and demonstrably do produce specialists; the particularistic-to-universalistic bridge names something real that every child crossing from home to school has to learn. The functionalist problems start where the description becomes a defence, and two are examinable on sight. First, <strong>whose norms</strong>: the "shared" values transmitted are, in a class- and ethnically-stratified society, disproportionately the values of the groups who staff and govern the schools — the transmission may be solidarity for some and estrangement for others (Bernstein, page 7, is the detailed version). Second, <strong>the bridge's toll</strong>: if the crossing from particularism to universalism is genuinely made, outcomes should not predict so exactly from the starting bank — the attainment gap is the bridge charging, and functionalism has no internal account of why (that silence is what Marxism supplies).</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Explain Durkheim's two functions with a concrete example of each. (b) Explain Parsons's bridge, defining particularism and universalism. (c) Why is "whose norms?" the standing functionalist criticism, and whose account supplies its detail? (d) Why does the attainment gap embarrass the bridge metaphor specifically?</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Social solidarity \u2014 the school teaches the norms that bind a complex society\u2019s strangers together: uniform, assembly, shared timetable, cooperation in teams and \u201cqueue and bell\u201d discipline make citizens of household children; specialist skills \u2014 the division of labour needs standardised competence in bulk (scientists, administrators, trades), produced to a level no household can: literacy and numeracy first, then the differentiating curriculum. (b) The family socialises particularistically (valued as someone\u2019s own, judged by no standard but love) while society operates universalistically (judged by impersonal, shared standards that do not know your name); school is the bridge between them, teaching the child to live under achieved rather than ascribed status \u2014 to be valued for what she does against rules that apply to everyone. (c) Because the \u201cshared\u201d values the school transmits are in fact disproportionately the values of the groups who staff and govern it, so the transmission is solidarity for some and estrangement for others; Bernstein\u2019s account of language codes and whose code the school rewards supplies the detailed mechanism. (d) Because the bridge\u2019s promise is that crossing it puts household background behind you \u2014 universalistic standards replacing the ascription of birth; but outcomes predict strongly from that background, meaning the bridge charges according to the bank you started from, and functionalism\u2019s own metaphor then needs an explanation (class-differentiated outcomes under formally equal rules) that the theory has no mechanism to give.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 3, title: "Functionalism II: Davis, Moore and the meritocratic sifting",
content: `
<p>The functionalist account completes itself with <strong>role allocation</strong> — Davis and Moore's "Some Principles of Stratification" (1945), the argument that unequal rewards are not only inevitable but <em>functional</em>. Some positions in society matter more than others (the surgeon matters more than the shelf-stacker) and take longer to train for, so society must both motivate the ablest into them and reward them for staying — and it needs an institution that finds the ablest regardless of where they start. That institution is school: education sifts and sorts by demonstrated ability, so the most important jobs go to the most capable people, and the unequal outcomes the sifting produces are, in the theory's boldest sentence, the mechanism by which everyone benefits.</p>
<p>This is the purest statement of <strong>meritocracy</strong> (Michael Young's 1958 satire gave the theory its name while mocking it) — and the claim that the attainment gap exists to test. The theory makes a falsifiable prediction: if school is sifting by ability, then attainment should predict from ability, and only through ability — children's outcomes should be independent of their parents' class once ability is held constant. The record says otherwise. Measured at school entry, class differences in attainment are already visible before any sifting has occurred; they widen rather than shrink across school years (meaning school adds more gap on top of the gap it inherited); and controlling for measured ability, class still predicts outcomes — the "residual" of class over ability is one of the most stable findings in the sociology of education. As Lesson 11 taught: the pattern is demonstrated; the functionalist explanation of it is, at best, the defensible pile; the claim that the sifting measures merit alone is the overreach pile.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #b45309;background:#fffbeb;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>A common misconception:</strong> "functionalism says the gap is fine." It says something more precise and more examinable: the gap is <em>functional if and only if</em> it tracks ability from any starting point — which the evidence contradicts three ways (gap before school, gap growing through school, class surviving ability-controls). The correct evaluation credits functionalism's insights (selection and differentiation are real social necessities) and then names the exact condition its central claim fails.</p>
</div>
<p>The theory's defenders have real moves, and a top-band answer plays them. Tumin's classic critique asks functional (for whom?) — rewards also motivate those who never receive them to accept their place, which is a legitimation function functionalism did not list. And the "importance" of positions is partly circular: surgeons are paid like surgeons partly because the profession controls its own supply — the rewards are produced by the very inequality the theory says rewards merely reflect. But the strongest modern functionalist line survives: differentiation must happen somewhere (any complex society needs its ablest in its hardest jobs), so the question is not whether to sift but whether the sifting is honest — and there the evidence damns the theory's optimism while sparing its core. Hold both: the necessity is real, the honesty is unproven; that is the two-sentence functionalist verdict the essays want.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Outline Davis and Moore's argument in three steps. (b) State the falsifiable prediction role allocation makes, and the three findings that contradict it. (c) Explain Tumin's "functional for whom?" critique. (d) Write the two-sentence functionalist verdict.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Positions differ in their importance to society and in the training they demand; the most important positions must be filled by the most able, whatever their origins, so society must motivate ability into them \u2014 unequal rewards are the incentive structure that gets the ablest trained and kept in the most functionally important roles; education is the sifting instrument that finds the ablest and allocates them. (b) Prediction: once ability is held constant, outcomes should not vary with class background \u2014 the sifting should be origin-blind; against it: class differences in attainment are measurable at school entry (before sifting), they widen across school years (school adds gap to inherited gap), and class predicts outcomes even after controlling for measured ability \u2014 the residual of class over ability is among the most stable findings in the field. (c) Rewards do not only motivate the able into important roles \u2014 they also motivate the many who never receive them to accept the inequality as deserved, so the unequal reward structure functions to legitimise stratification for those it fails, a legitimation function the theory never lists because its functionalism is written from the top\u2019s point of view; and position-\u201cimportance\u201d is partly circular, since professions partly produce their own scarcity and thus their own rewards. (d) Differentiation is a real necessity \u2014 any complex society must identify ability and train it; but the honesty of school\u2019s sifting is unproven \u2014 entry gaps, widening through school and class residuals after ability-controls show the sifting measures advantage as well as ability, so the functionalist account of <em>what school must do</em> stands while its account of <em>what school actually does</em> fails its own test.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 4, title: "Marxism I: Althusser's apparatus and Bowles and Gintis's correspondence",
content: `
<p>Marxism reads the same institution from underneath: school is not society's answer to cohesion but the ruling class's answer to labour supply. <strong>Louis Althusser</strong> (1970) named education the dominant <strong>ideological state apparatus</strong> (ISAs being the institutions — church, media, family, school — that reproduce ruling ideas by consent, where the repressive apparatuses — police, army — do it by force). School does the ideological work in two registers at once: it transmits the ruling ideology — respect for hierarchy, obedience to impersonal authority, the naturalness of one's "place" — wrapped so thoroughly in neutral knowledge that no one sees the wrapping; and it reproduces the class structure itself, sorting the children of each class toward that class's destinations, generation after generation, while calling the sorting fair. The examinable core: the ISA explains why the reproduction is <em>consensual</em> — the ideology is inside the timetable, so no one has to be persuaded at gunpoint.</p>
<p><strong>Samuel Bowles and Herbert Gintis</strong>, <em>Schooling in Capitalist America</em> (1976), supplied the mechanism with the specification's most quotable term: the <strong>correspondence principle</strong>. School's social relations correspond to workplace relations point for point: hierarchy (headteacher–teacher–pupil as manager–supervisor–worker); external rewards (grades as wages — motivation kept external, never for its own sake); fragmentation (subjects chopped into graded pieces as tasks into deskilled jobs); and punctuality, dress and deference policed as they will be in the workforce. The <strong>hidden curriculum</strong> — everything school teaches without teaching it, from the register to the reward chart — is where the correspondence lives. And the piece that makes the whole system run: the <strong>myth of meritocracy</strong>, their phrase for the belief that outcomes reflect effort and ability, which does the crucial ideological job of blaming the failed rather than the failing system. Note the mirror to Lesson 7: the myth of meritocracy is Merton's strain with the ladder secretly crooked — the blocked means are misdescribed as open, and the blame lands on the blocked.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/wjec-as-sociology/wjec-soc-school-theories.png" alt="A two-column comparison of functionalist and Marxist theories of schooling. Left column, functionalism (blue): Durkheim — school transmits the shared norms that hold a complex society together and teaches the specialist skills the division of labour needs; Parsons — school is the bridge between family particularism (a child loved whatever) and society's universalism (achievement judged by shared standards); Davis and Moore — schools sift and sort by ability so the most able fill the most important jobs, and meritocracy justifies the unequal rewards; limitation — whose merit? the class gap between attainment at entry and exit says the sifting is not measuring ability alone. Right column, Marxism (red): Althusser — education is the ideological state apparatus that reproduces ruling-class ideas (obedience, hierarchy, your place) while pretending to transmit neutral knowledge; Bowles and Gintis — the correspondence principle: school mirrors the workplace in hierarchy, external rewards and fragmentation, the hidden curriculum produces the worker capitalism requires, and the myth of meritocracy blames those it fails; Willis — resistance: the working-class lads saw through the meritocratic myth, and their counter-school culture delivered them into exactly the jobs the system had waiting; limitation — determinism: if school simply reproduces capitalism, no reform, no teaching and no individual escape could happen, and Willis's own lads show pupils reading the school against it. Bottom band: functionalism explains what school does for society, Marxism explains what society does through school; every later question — the gap, labelling, policy — is a test of which account the evidence fits." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">The two structural theories side by side. The band's asymmetry is worth memorising: functionalism's test is the honest sifting of merit (which the entry-and-exit gap fails), and Marxism's test is reproduction (which Willis's resistance complicates). Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Define an ideological state apparatus and explain why Althusser's reproduction is consensual. (b) Outline the correspondence principle with three point-for-point parallels. (c) How does the myth of meritocracy mirror Merton's strain theory? (d) What does "the ideology is inside the timetable" mean, and why is it the examinable core of Althusser?</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) An ISA is one of the institutions (education, church, family, media) that reproduce ruling ideology and the class structure by consent rather than force, as against the repressive apparatuses that coerce; the reproduction is consensual because the ideas are transmitted wrapped inside neutral, useful knowledge \u2014 hierarchy, obedience and \u201cyour place\u201d arrive as the timetable\u2019s ordinary grammar, so nobody is argued into them at gunpoint: consent is manufactured in the curriculum\u2019s own form. (b) Hierarchy \u2014 headteacher, teacher, pupil mirrors manager, supervisor, worker, teaching deference to impersonal authority; external rewards \u2014 grades motivate as wages motivate, keeping motivation external rather than intrinsic, as the workplace will require; fragmentation \u2014 knowledge chopped into graded, examined pieces mirrors work deskilled into measured tasks; plus punctuality, dress and deference policed as in the workforce, the whole correspondence living in the hidden curriculum. (c) Merton\u2019s strain is the gap between culturally installed goals and unequally distributed legitimate means; the myth of meritocracy is strain theory with the means falsely described as open \u2014 success goals installed in all, the ladder quietly crooked, and the resulting failure blamed on the failed rather than the system, which is the ideological work Merton\u2019s conformists never had to do. (d) It means the ruling ideology is carried by school\u2019s ordinary forms \u2014 the register, the ranking, the reward chart, the timetable\u2019s obedience training \u2014 not by explicit teaching that anyone could dispute; it is Althusser\u2019s examinable core because it explains the mechanism (reproduction without propaganda) and it directs the evaluator to the hidden curriculum, where the claim can actually be checked.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 5, title: "Marxism II: Willis's lads — resistance that reproduces",
content: `
<p>Paul Willis's <em>Learning to Labour</em> (1977) is the study the whole structural debate turns on, and WJEC expects it whole. Willis followed twelve working-class "lads" through their last years of a Midlands secondary school — a qualitative study in Unit 2's own toolkit (participant observation and group interviews, Lesson 10's methods applied), and therefore examinable as method as well as finding. The lads, like Albert Cohen's boys of Lesson 7, saw through the school's promise — and this is Willis's decisive break with reproduction theory: they <em>knew</em> the meritocratic myth was not for them. Their <strong>counter-school culture</strong> — dossing, having a laff, rejecting the "ear'oles" (the conformists) and the teachers' rules — was a working-class culture of masculinity transplanted into the classroom, a genuine act of resistance with real insight into the inequality of the game they were made to play.</p>
<p>But the study's title is a pun, and the second half is the finding: their resistance delivered them into exactly the destination the system had waiting. By misrecognising manual work — seeing in the factory floor the same freedom, banter and masculinity their counter-school culture prized — the lads walked with open eyes into the low-paid, low-security jobs that reproduction theory predicted, but by a route it could not describe: not through false consciousness but through <strong>partial insight plus class-cultural misrecognition</strong>. Willis's phrase for the lads' penetration of the ideology (their seeing-through) is balanced against its limitation — their critique of the school stops short of a critique of the labour market that rewards it. The one-line version, to quote exactly: <em>resistance to school that reproduces the class structure anyway</em>.</p>
<p>The evaluation must run in both directions, because the study's methods (Lesson 10 again) cut both ways. Strengths: it explained the mechanism reproduction theory had merely asserted (how class destinations are produced, from the inside, in the actors' own words) — analytic generalisation of the mechanism at its best (Lesson 10, page 7); it showed pupils as active readers of school, not passive recipients of ideology; it connected classroom culture to shop-floor culture in one continuous account. Weaknesses: twelve boys in one school (transferability argued by thick description, not sample size — and modern Britain's destinations and labour markets have changed); no girls (the parallel question — how class and gender together organised the lads' counter-culture and their female contemporaries' invisibility — is the standing feminist criticism, Lesson 7's McRobbie applied); and the sociologist's own position as an observing adult in the counter-culture was the participant-observer's standing problem (Lesson 10, page 6).</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #1d4ed8;background:#eff6ff;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>Willis in the essay, one sentence each:</strong> against functionalism — pupils are not naively socialised; the lads read the school against its ideology, so value consensus is not given. Against reproduction Marxism — ideology is not simply poured in; the lads resist it and still arrive, so the mechanism is cultural, not mechanical. For the whole debate — the finding that survives both readings: class destinations are produced <em>with</em> the pupil's participation, through meanings, not merely imposed on it.</p>
</div>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Outline Willis's counter-school culture and its two components he most stresses. (b) Explain the title's pun: how does resistance reproduce? (c) Run the methods evaluation (strengths and weaknesses) on the study. (d) State the three essay sentences Willis serves for, in each direction.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) A working-class masculine culture transplanted into the classroom: dossing, having a laff, rejecting conformity (the \u201cear\u2019oles\u201d) and teacher authority; Willis most stresses its <em>penetration</em> \u2014 the lads\u2019 real insight that the meritocratic promise was not for them \u2014 and its misrecognition: reading manual work as the same freedom, banter and masculinity the counter-culture prized. (b) The lads resist the school\u2019s ideology with partial insight rather than false consciousness, yet their counter-culture equips and motivates them for exactly the manual, low-paid jobs the system had waiting \u2014 the resistance itself conducts them into reproduction: they learn to labour by refusing to learn, so the class structure is reproduced <em>through</em> their agency, not despite it. (c) Strengths: it supplied the mechanism reproduction theory asserted, in the actors\u2019 own categories (analytic generalisation); it showed pupils as active readers of school; it joined classroom to shop-floor in one account. Weaknesses: twelve boys in one Midlands school (transferability argued by thick description; destinations and labour markets have changed since the 1970s); no girls \u2014 the class-and-gender question of the lads\u2019 female contemporaries is the standing feminist criticism; and the adult observer\u2019s position inside a counter-school culture carried the familiar participant-observation limits. (d) Against functionalism: pupils are not naively socialised \u2014 the lads read school against its ideology, so value consensus is given to no one. Against reproduction Marxism: ideology is not poured into passive vessels \u2014 the lads resist it and still arrive, so the mechanism is cultural and active, not mechanical. For the debate\u2019s future: class destinations are produced with the pupil\u2019s participation, through meanings the school neither fully controls nor fully wrote.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 6, title: "The class attainment gap: the pattern and its measurements",
content: `
<p>The option's empirical spine: the gap in recorded attainment between children eligible for free school meals (the standing proxy for family poverty) and the rest. The pattern's headline facts, stated with Lesson 11's discipline. The gap is <strong>large</strong>: at GCSE, the proportion of FSM pupils achieving the standard benchmark has run persistently far below non-FSM pupils, with decades of policy leaving a substantial difference standing. The gap is <strong>early</strong>: class differences in cognitive and language measures are visible before school even begins — the inheritance arrives before any sifting (page 3's first contradiction of role allocation). The gap <strong>widens</strong> across school years: whatever schools are doing, the relative positions spread rather than converge — the institution adds difference on top of the difference it receives (the second contradiction). And the gap is <strong>resilient</strong>: controlling for measured ability, class still predicts — the residual that no sifting theory survives.</p>
<p>Now the measurements, because every number above is a construct and the evaluation expects you to know how. "Attainment" means whatever the year's benchmark counts — 5 A*-C in one era, 9-4 passes in the next, combined measures with English and maths after that — so the gap's <em>size</em> jumps with every redefinition (Lesson 11's category-change problem, at national scale). "FSM" is a narrow administrative category (benefit-entitled, currently registered), missing the near-poor and the unregistered, so the measured gap is the gap of the <em>counted</em> poor. And the school-level figures select on entries: a school's results describe the pupils it entered for the examinations, so comparisons between schools partly compare entry policies (Lesson 9's denominator problem). None of this dissolves the pattern — which replicates across decades, datasets and measures — but each caveat earns the evaluation its top band, because the next pages' <em>explanations</em> must explain a gap that is partly real and partly measured.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #1d4ed8;background:#eff6ff;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>The pattern in four words:</strong> large (persistent across decades), early (visible pre-school), widening (grows through school years), resilient (survives ability controls). Each word defeats a different account: large defeats "reform has solved it"; early defeats pure school-failure explanations; widening indicts the school as well as the home; resilient indicts the sifting theory. The explanations of pages 7–8 must fit all four facts, not just the headline.</p>
</div>
<p>Why the gap is the pivot of the whole option: it is the empirical fact that every theory on pages 2–5 must meet. Functionalism predicted origin-blind sifting — the early, widening, resilient gap denies it. Marxism predicted class reproduction — the gap's generation-to-generation stability supports it, while Willis (page 5) forces the mechanism to run through meanings rather than machinery. And the interactionist pages that follow supply the school-side mechanism functionalism lacked and reproduction theory needed: the gap is not only inherited from home and imposed by structure; it is <em>produced in classrooms</em>, day by day, through labels and placements. That three-level story — home, classroom, structure — is the option's master answer, and the diagram on page 7 is its map.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) State the gap's four facts and say which account each defeats. (b) Give three measurement caveats on the gap's headline numbers, in Lesson 11's vocabulary. (c) Why does each theory on pages 2–5 have to meet the gap? (d) What is the "three-level story" of the option?</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Large \u2014 defeats \u201creform has solved it\u201d: a substantial gap stands after decades of policy; early \u2014 defeats pure school-failure accounts: difference arrives before schooling, so school cannot be its sole author; widening \u2014 indicts the school alongside the home: school years spread the relative positions rather than closing them, so the institution adds gap; resilient \u2014 indicts the sifting theory: class predicts outcomes after ability controls, so the sorting is not measuring merit alone. (b) \u201cAttainment\u201d is the year\u2019s benchmark, so the gap\u2019s measured size jumps with each redefinition (category change breaking trend lines); \u201cFSM\u201d is a narrow administrative category (currently registered benefit entitlement), so the measured gap is the counted poor\u2019s gap, missing the near-poor and unregistered; school-level figures describe entered pupils, so between-school comparisons partly compare entry policies (denominator selection) \u2014 yet the pattern replicates across datasets and measures, so the caveats qualify rather than dissolve it. (c) Because the gap is the outcome every theory of schooling is a theory of: functionalism\u2019s origin-blind sifting, Marxism\u2019s class reproduction and Willis\u2019s cultural mechanism each predict a different shape for the gap \u2014 absent, mechanically stable, or produced-through-meanings \u2014 and the four facts adjudicate among them. (d) The three-level master account: the gap is inherited from home (material and cultural differences), produced in classrooms (labelling, expectations, placements \u2014 the mechanism the next pages give), and structured by policy and selection (what counts as choice and funding) \u2014 the levels interlock rather than compete, and the mature essay runs all three.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 7, title: "Explaining the gap I: the home — material and cultural accounts",
content: `
<p>The first family of explanations locates the gap's cause in the circumstances children bring through the school gate. Its <strong>material</strong> wing is the plainest: poverty costs attainment directly. Overcrowded housing means nowhere to study; a cold flat and poor diet mean missed schooling and missed concentration; no transport means narrow horizons of school choice; no resources means no quiet laptop, no books, no residential trips — the curriculum's unspoken accessories. The tradition's founding demonstration was J. W. B. Douglas's longitudinal study (1964), following the 1946 birth cohort: material circumstances in the primary years predicted secondary destinations as strongly as measured ability — the first great statistical demonstration that the ladder's outcomes were set partly before the climbing started. In the modern record, the material wing's standing evidence is the stubbornness of the FSM gap across every decade of reform: money, however the measures are dressed, still tracks attainment.</p>
<p>The <strong>cultural</strong> wing argues that the deeper inheritance is not cash but <em>capital</em>. Pierre Bourdieu's account has three linked moves. <strong>Cultural capital</strong>: the educated home transmits language, tastes, confidence and the taken-for-granted "feel for the game" that school rewards — not as extras but as the school's own currency, so that its "meritocratic" measures already measure home. <strong>The habitus</strong>: the durable dispositions a class position instils — what feels natural to aim for, fear, value or despise — so that middle-class children arrive finding school's expectations familiar and some working-class children find them foreign territory. And <strong>field and symbolic violence</strong>: in the school's field, one class's culture is the standard against which all are judged, and the judgement presents itself as the pupil's own failure — the gap reframed as gift-difference, not standard-difference. Basil Bernstein's language-codes argument is the micro-version: the school operates in the <strong>elaborated code</strong> (explicit, context-independent, written-formal), which is the middle-class home's register; the <strong>restricted code</strong> of close, local, oral communities is not deficient but <em>different</em> — and school tests, without saying so, are code tests.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/wjec-as-sociology/wjec-soc-attainment-gap.png" alt="A three-panel map of explanations of the class attainment gap, with an essay-guidance band. Panel 1, home: material and cultural (blue): material deprivation — income, housing, space, diet, transport, resources: poverty costs attainment directly (Douglas's 1946-cohort study as founding demonstration); cultural capital — Bourdieu: the educated home transmits the language, tastes and confidence school rewards, so the meritocratic yardstick already measures home advantage; language codes — Bernstein: the school's elaborated code is the middle-class one, and its tests are code tests. Panel 2, school: processes and practice (red): labelling and expectations — Becker's ideal pupil, Rosenthal and Jacobson's bloomers: the school does not just measure gaps, it widens them; setting and tiering — the gap is structured into groups, tiers of entry and option blocks, placements following behaviour and background as much as attainment; curriculum and assessment — whose knowledge is examined, and how: a contest hidden inside the yardstick itself. Panel 3, policy: structure and choice (green): selection and marketisation — quasi-markets, league tables and parental choice sort pupils and schools; the parentocracy lets middle-class advantage operate as choice (Gewirtz's choosers); resourcing and area effects — funding formulas, catchments and house prices: the purchase of a school place through the property market; compensatory policy — its assumption (home is the problem) is itself the argument: do the panels fail the school, or does the school fail the pupil? Bottom band, how the essay decides, the three-step move: step 1, the pattern is real and enormous, the descriptive layer no panel contests; step 2, the panels are not rivals but levels — home advantage is real, and school converts it into attainment through labels, placements and yardsticks, while policy decides both; step 3, the verdict is evidence-weighed, not balanced — compensatory policies that assume home alone leave the school's own widening untouched, and the interactionist evidence is the empirical reason home-only accounts fail. Pattern, levels, verdict: the examination's most repeatable essay shape." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">The gap map: home, school and policy panels. The band's three-step move (pattern \u2192 levels \u2192 verdict) is the option's most transferable essay shape — and its honest verdict is always evidence-weighed, never a balanced shrug. Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The <strong>criticisms</strong> travel with the wing. The material account is true but partial: within any income band, attainment still varies by home cultural practice, which is Bourdieu's point; and compensation programmes (breakfast, laptops, pupil premium) have reliably produced smaller effects than the deprivation narrative predicted — the money helps, and the gap stands, so money is not the whole of home. The cultural account's standing criticism is the one Bourdieu half-welcomed: read carelessly, "cultural capital" becomes deficit theory — code-switching talk becomes "poor homes lack language", which is both empirically false (restricted-code talk is rich, locally) and politically convenient (blaming the home, Lesson 8's gatekeeper problem in theory form). The careful version, which the essays should use, keeps the mechanism on the school side of the boundary: <em>the home's difference only becomes a gap where the school rewards one class's version of it</em> — which is exactly where the next page's story begins.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Outline the material wing, naming Douglas's study and one modern standing evidence. (b) Explain Bourdieu's cultural capital and habitus, then Bernstein's codes. (c) Why is "cultural deficit" the careless reading of the cultural account, and what is the careful version? (d) What does the modest effect of compensation programmes tell both wings?</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Poverty costs attainment directly: no study space in overcrowded housing, concentration lost to cold and diet, narrow choice horizons without transport, and the unspoken curriculum accessories \u2014 laptop, books, trips \u2014 unaffordable; Douglas\u2019s 1946-cohort longitudinal study showed primary-years material circumstances predicting secondary destinations as strongly as measured ability, the founding demonstration that outcomes are partly set before school; the modern standing evidence is the FSM gap\u2019s persistence through every decade of reform \u2014 money still tracks attainment. (b) Cultural capital: the educated home transmits the language, tastes and confidence that school rewards as its own currency, so meritocratic measures already measure home; habitus: the durable dispositions a class position installs \u2014 what feels natural to aim at or fear \u2014 so middle-class children find school familiar while others find it foreign; Bernstein: the school\u2019s elaborated code (explicit, context-free, formal-register) is the middle-class home\u2019s code, so its tests are code tests that convert a class difference of register into a measured gap. (c) Carelessly, cultural capital reads as deficit theory: \u201cpoor homes lack language\u201d \u2014 empirically false (restricted-code talk is rich locally) and politically convenient, blaming the home; carefully, the mechanism is on the school\u2019s side: the home\u2019s difference only becomes a gap where the school rewards one class\u2019s version of it, so the explanatory weight falls on the standard and its rewards, not the deficit. (d) That money helps but the gap stands \u2014 compensation\u2019s modest returns show material deprivation is real but not the whole of home, and equally that the gap is not all home: the school-side widening (page 6\u2019s second fact) survives compensation, which is the standing empirical case for adding the classroom\u2019s own mechanisms (page 8) to any explanation.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 8, title: "Explaining the gap II: the classroom — labels, expectations and prophecies",
content: `
<p>The second family of explanations locates part of the gap's production inside the school itself — not as conspiracy but as interaction. Its founding study is Howard Becker's <em>Classrooms</em> (1971), whose finding is one sentence: teachers classify pupils, and the classification is class-tinted. The "ideal pupil" in the interviewed teachers' descriptions was a middle-class construction — neat, polite, well-spoken, deferential — so the working-class child arrives already less ideal on grounds that are style, not attainment; and the label, once applied, is not a description but an input. This is Lesson 7's labelling theory (Becker again — the same theorist, the deviance chapter's), transplanted from crime to classroom, and the specification wants exactly that continuity named.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/wjec-as-sociology/wjec-soc-labelling-chain.png" alt="A six-node loop diagram of the interactionist chain in classrooms. Node 1, the label: teachers classify pupils from the evidence they meet first — Becker (1971): the ideal pupil is a middle-class construct, and dress, speech and manner all enter the classification. Node 2, the expectation: the label becomes a settled expectation of ability and behaviour — streaming and setting turn it into structure (which class, which table, which tier of entry). Node 3, the self-fulfilling prophecy: Rosenthal and Jacobson (1968) — pupils live up to or down to what is expected; the randomly-labelled bloomers bloomed, because expectation changed how they were taught and how they saw themselves. Node 4, differentiation and polarisation: Lacey (1970) — the school differentiates (ranks by achievement) and pupils polarise around the ranking: pro-school and anti-school identities are its two stable responses. Node 5, pupil subcultures: Hargreaves (1967) and Abraham (1989) — the sets of values and behaviours a group shares: pro-school conforming, anti-school inverting (dossing, messing, having a laff); Willis's lads are its classic case. Node 6, back to the label: the subculture's behaviour confirms the teacher's original classification — the circle closes, and the interaction, not the original ability, has produced the outcome. Bottom band: the interactionist's two limits and the structuralist's bridge: interactionism explains how a label becomes an outcome but not where the labels come from — Becker's ideal pupil being middle-class is a class structure operating through a teacher's head; the complete account is two-storey: the structural theories explain the conditions, the interactionist chain explains the mechanism, and the strongest essays run both: class supplies the labels, the classroom turns them into attainment." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">The labelling chain, node by node. The band is the essay's structure in one sentence: <em>class supplies the labels, the classroom turns them into attainment</em> — the interactionist mechanism nested inside the structural account, which is how the option's three-level story is written. Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The chain's full specification version runs through four named studies, all of which Lesson 9 or 11 has already taught you to evaluate. The <strong>self-fulfilling prophecy</strong>: Rosenthal and Jacobson's <em>Pygmalion in the Classroom</em> (1968) — pupils labelled at random as "bloomers" made real gains, because the label changed the teaching they received; a field experiment (Lesson 9, page 6), with everything that means: strong causal direction, real-setting evidence, and the deception ethics the study is still asked about. The <strong>differentiation and polarisation</strong> that structures the prophecy: David Lacey (1970) — schools rank (differentiation), and pupils organise their identities around the ranking (polarisation), so the pro-school and anti-school worlds form <em>as responses to the school's own sorting</em>. And the <strong>subcultures</strong>: David Hargreaves (1967) on the streams' distinct cultures, and David Abraham (1989) updating the chain with step-by-step evidence of pupils' destinations by stream. Willis's lads (page 5) are the anti-school culture's classic case, and the loop's cruellest: the subculture's behaviour confirms the original label, so the interaction, not the initial ability, produced the outcome.</p>
<p>The evaluation, in the two halves the top band runs. Interactionism's strength is the mechanism: it explains page 6's second and third facts (the gap <em>widens</em> through school years — labels and placements at work; the class residual after ability controls — style read as ability) with named, checkable processes. Its limits are the band's first sentence: labels do not fall from the sky — Becker's ideal pupil is <em>middle-class</em>, which means the class structure is operating through the teacher's head, and an account of the classroom that stops at the classroom door cannot see it. The strongest essays therefore run the chain as the middle floor of the three-storey story: home equips differently, classroom converts difference into rank, policy structures the market the ranks are traded in. And on policy: the interactionist evidence is precisely why the compensatory assumption of page 7 (home is the problem) is incomplete — breakfast programmes cannot unlabel a child.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Outline Becker's ideal pupil finding and its link to Lesson 7. (b) Run the chain from prophecy to subculture with the four named studies. (c) Explain how the chain accounts for the gap's widening and its class residual. (d) State the interactionist limits and the "middle floor" resolution.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Teachers classify pupils from first evidence, and the \u201cideal pupil\u201d in their descriptions is a middle-class construct \u2014 neat, polite, well-spoken, deferential \u2014 so working-class children arrive less ideal on grounds of style rather than attainment; the link to Lesson 7 is the same theorist and theory: Becker\u2019s labelling (deviance is a label applied by audiences with power) transplanted from crime to classroom \u2014 the classroom label works exactly as the deviance label does, as an input that organises the response rather than a description of what was there. (b) The label becomes a settled expectation, and setting and streaming turn it into structure (tier, table, class); the prophecy does the rest \u2014 Rosenthal and Jacobson\u2019s randomly-labelled bloomers gained because expectation changed the teaching they received; Lacey\u2019s differentiation (ranking) and polarisation (identities forming around the ranking) explains why the responses organise into pro- and anti-school worlds; Hargreaves\u2019 and Abraham\u2019s subcultures are those worlds\u2019 contents, with Willis\u2019 lads the anti-school classic \u2014 and the subculture\u2019s behaviour then confirms the original label, closing the loop. (c) Widening: the gap grows through school years because the school is not passively receiving difference but actively converting style into rank \u2014 labels, placements and expectations add school-made gap on top of inherited difference; class residual: after ability controls, class still predicts because \u201cability\u201d as read by teachers includes the middle-class style of the ideal pupil, so the sifting measures dress as well as attainment \u2014 both facts are exactly what a mechanism of labels and placements predicts and a pure home account cannot produce. (d) Limits: the chain explains how a label becomes an outcome but not where the labels come from \u2014 Becker\u2019s ideal pupil being middle-class is the class structure operating through teachers\u2019 heads, invisible to an account that stops at the classroom door, and the studies\u2019 own designs (one school, small samples) bound their transferability; resolution: the chain is the middle floor of the three-storey story \u2014 home equips differently, classroom converts difference into rank, policy structures the market the ranks are traded in \u2014 class supplies the labels; the classroom turns them into attainment.</p></div></details>` },
];